152 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 539 0 R
/E (Abstract: Lung diseases remain a pressing global health challenge, requiring both scientific insight into research evolution and practical advances in diagnostic solutions. To address this, the present study integrates bibliometric analysis with experimental validation of computational models, offering a combined medical and technical perspective. Research articles were retrieved from Web of Science Core Collection, Scopus, PubMed, Dimensions.ai, IEEE Xplore, and Google Scholar, and systematically analyzed using VOSviewer and CiteSpace to identify publication trajectories, research hotspots, and institutional collaborations. Drawing upon these bibliometric insights, deep learning models for three-dimensional lung nodule detection were developed and applied to low-dose CT scans obtained from 605 cancer patients. Model performance was assessed using sensitivity, Dice coefficient, and false positive rates. The bibliometric results indicated sustained growth in lung disease research, highlighting radiomics and AI-driven methodologies as emerging focal points, alongside significant international cooperation. On the experimental side, the models achieved a peak Dice score of 0.9220, demonstrating strong sensitivity for small nodules and reduced false detections when compared with conventional baselines. By bridging large-scale research mapping with empirical validation on real-world patient data, this study underscores both the scientific directions shaping lung disease research and the clinical utility of advanced computational approaches, thereby providing valuable guidance to researchers, technologists, and healthcare practitioners alike.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [17]
>>
