165 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 547 0 R
/E (Abstract: Ablution, the act of cleaning oneself before performing prayers. However, this process requires repetitive motion of various parts of body and the disabled people, especially the elderly, face challenges while performing this ritual by themselves. This paper introduces an ablution device, a machine that combines practicality, accessibility, and water-saving characteristics. The tool accommodates people with various body shapes, heights, and mobility disabilities by applying universal design principles. The inclusiveness is ensured by the ergonomic design, which tackles upper body constraints. Portability and sensory accessibility were also taken into consideration, making it usable for individuals having sensory limitations. Water-saving techniques are used to encourage conservation. With the help of Auto-CAD a three-dimensional model of the machine was also produced. The design effectively prompts and offers feedback to help with task completion. The implementation of this device on a bigger scale could enable people with disabilities to perform ablution with ease and save water.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
